%=========================================================
\section{Scientific Questions and Hypotheses}
%=========================================================

The apparent contradiction between microscopic reversibility and macroscopic
irreversibility has long been a central problem in statistical mechanics and
dynamical astronomy. Collisionless stellar systems obey Hamiltonian dynamics,
for which Liouville's theorem guarantees conservation of fine-grained
phase-space density. Nevertheless, real galaxies undergo processes such as
violent relaxation, phase mixing, and secular heating, all of which exhibit a
clear arrow of time.

Galaxy mergers provide a natural laboratory for studying this tension. They
involve strong, time-dependent gravitational potentials, rapid orbital
rearrangement, and large-scale redistribution of orbital actions, all while
remaining formally collisionless. The Milky Way--Andromeda merger, in
particular, offers a physically motivated yet idealisable system in which
irreversibility can be examined in a controlled setting.

This work addresses the following scientific questions:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Can macroscopic irreversibility be quantified in a collisionless
    N-body system evolved with a formally time-reversible, symplectic
    integrator?
    \item Does coarse-grained phase-space information diverge monotonically
    during a major galaxy merger?
    \item To what extent is observed irreversibility sensitive to the choice of
    coarse-graining scale and numerical resolution?
    \item How does the growth of phase-space divergence relate to physically
    observable consequences such as stellar disk heating?
\end{itemize}

We hypothesize that while fine-grained phase-space density remains conserved in
principle, coarse-grained phase-space measures will exhibit systematic
divergence during the merger phase. Furthermore, we expect this divergence to
correlate with irreversible increases in stellar velocity dispersion, even in
the absence of dissipation, feedback, or stochastic forces.

%=========================================================
\section{Experimental Design}
%=========================================================

The numerical experiments are designed to isolate physical sources of
irreversibility from numerical artifacts. All simulations employ identical
numerical schemes, force solvers, and integration parameters unless explicitly
varied for sensitivity studies.

Three classes of simulations are performed:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Isolated Control Simulations}: A Milky Way--like disk--halo
    system evolved in isolation to establish baseline numerical diffusion,
    phase-space drift, and energy conservation properties.
    \item \textbf{Merger Simulations}: A head-on Milky Way--Andromeda merger
    initialized with a separation of 780~kpc and an initial relative velocity
    of 120~km~s$^{-1}$, chosen to approximate a bound encounter while remaining
    computationally tractable.
    \item \textbf{Resolution and Coarse-Graining Tests}: Repeated merger runs
    using different phase-space binning resolutions to assess the dependence
    of irreversibility measures on coarse-graining scale.
\end{enumerate}

All simulations are evolved forward in time using a fixed timestep leapfrog
integrator. Snapshots of the full six-dimensional phase-space state are recorded
at regular intervals and stored for post-processing. No attempt is made to
re-equilibrate the system during evolution; deviations from equilibrium are
treated as physically meaningful drivers of phase mixing and secular evolution.

%=========================================================
\section{Diagnostics and Observables}
%=========================================================

\subsection{Coarse-Grained Phase-Space Irreversibility}

At each stored snapshot, the full six-dimensional phase-space distribution is
constructed from particle positions and velocities. This distribution is
coarse-grained into a fixed number of bins along each dimension, producing a
normalized phase-space density function $P(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{v})$.

Macroscopic irreversibility is quantified using the Kullback--Leibler divergence
between an initial reference distribution $P_0$ and a time-evolved distribution
$P_t$:
\begin{equation}
    D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_0 || P_t) =
    \sum_i P_{0,i} \ln \left( \frac{P_{0,i}}{P_{t,i}} \right).
\end{equation}

The KL divergence measures the distinguishability of two probability
distributions under coarse-graining. An increase in $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ reflects
loss of information about the initial phase-space configuration, even when the
underlying microscopic dynamics remain reversible.

\subsection{Stellar Disk Heating}

Disk heating is quantified using the vertical velocity dispersion,
\begin{equation}
    \sigma_z^2 = \langle v_z^2 \rangle - \langle v_z \rangle^2,
\end{equation}
which provides a physically intuitive measure of disk thickening and orbital
decoherence. This diagnostic is computed selectively for disk particles using
component identifiers, allowing isolation of disk-specific evolution from halo
dynamics.

In addition, the $z$-component of angular momentum is tracked on a per-particle
basis to assess the redistribution of orbital actions during the merger and its
relation to irreversible heating.

%=========================================================
\section{Validation and Numerical Consistency}
%=========================================================

To ensure that observed irreversibility is not an artifact of numerical error,
global conserved quantities are monitored throughout all simulations. Total
kinetic energy, potential energy, and angular momentum are computed at each
snapshot and compared against their initial values.

The leapfrog integrator employed is symplectic and time-reversible, ensuring
that the numerical scheme does not introduce explicit dissipation. Residual
energy drift remains small compared to the magnitude of phase-space divergence
observed during merger phases.

Isolated control simulations exhibit significantly reduced entropy growth and
disk heating relative to merger runs, confirming that the dominant source of
irreversibility arises from physical phase mixing driven by time-dependent
gravitational potentials rather than numerical diffusion alone. These tests
establish the reliability of the diagnostics and provide a baseline against
which merger-induced irreversibility can be assessed.
